      "justifications": {
        "disambiguation": "The descriptions are extraordinarily defensive, with explicit 'Use when / Do NOT use when' cross-references on nearly every tool \u2014 a strong signal that boundaries are genuinely fuzzy. Near-neighbors abound (manage_lore vs manage_note vs manage_corpus vs manage_belief, manage_countdown vs manage_vow, manage_staging vs manage_scene, open_session/close_session/take_turn/set_badge), though the routing hints do provide a workable disambiguation path.",
        "naming_consistency": "The dominant convention is manage_<noun> across ~20 tools, with a secondary verb-first set (set_badge, respond_decision, run_command, open_session, close_session, take_turn, resolve_mechanic) that is still predictable verb_noun form. The two families are discernible (resource CRUD vs. lifecycle actions), so minor blending but no chaos.",
        "tool_count_appropriateness": "36 tools far exceeds a comfortable surface for one server, and several are multi-action multiplexers (manage_diagnostics, manage_ruleset, manage_synthesis, manage_corpus, manage_archive) that each bundle 8-10 operations. The breadth also spills across genuinely separate concerns \u2014 play, archives, codex, rulesets, diagnostics \u2014 making discovery and selection costly.",
        "completeness": "The surface is remarkably thorough: world model, PCs/NPCs, factions, lore, story journal, conditions, combat, vows, clocks, beliefs, causality, corpus, rulesets, synthesis, archive/checkpoints, session lifecycle, and diagnostics are all covered with undo semantics. Only minor gaps exist, such as idempotent reversal for character/identity/vow mutations, which agents must work around by re-issuing state."
      },
